\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.1}{\ignorespaces A single year says little about how good the stock market is, because ordinary years range from large losses to large gains. The curve shows one year's excess return on the stock market, assuming an expected excess return of 7\% and volatility of 20\%. The blue band covers the middle two-thirds of years, from $-13$\% to 27\%; the gray area is the 36\% of years with a negative excess return.}}{21}{figure.caption.4}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.2}{\ignorespaces Borrowing can make any strategy earn more, but only by taking more risk, so the fair comparison is return per unit of risk. The Sharpe ratio is the slope of a line from the risk-free rate. Leverage moves a portfolio along its line, raising or lowering both excess return and volatility, but does not change the slope. A better strategy lies on a steeper line.}}{22}{figure.caption.5}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.3}{\ignorespaces The weaker a strategy's edge, the longer its record must be before luck can be ruled out. The curves show how many years of return data are needed before a strategy's track record becomes statistically significant. The years count the length of the history being analyzed, not how long the strategy must trade or hold its positions. With a true Sharpe ratio of 0.5, about 15 years of data are needed to reach $t = 1.96$. With a Sharpe ratio of 0.25, about 61 years are needed.}}{28}{figure.caption.6}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1.4}{\ignorespaces What a 10-year record can show. Even a decade of results cannot reliably tell a good strategy from a useless one, because the two ranges of outcomes overlap heavily. The gray curve is the range of Sharpe ratios a strategy with no skill produces over different decades, and the blue curve is the same for a strategy with a true Sharpe ratio of 0.5. The shaded tail is the 6\% of decades in which the useless strategy beats 0.5 by luck.}}{36}{figure.caption.8}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces The more an investment swings, the more its simple average return overstates what investors actually earned. Volatility drag: the gap between the arithmetic and geometric average returns, from Equation~\eqref {eq:02-drag}. The gap is negligible for Treasury bills, about 2 percentage points a year for the stock market and about 6 for small stocks, and it rises with the square of volatility.}}{53}{figure.caption.9}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.2}{\ignorespaces A backtest must wait until accounting numbers were actually public before trading on them. When accounting data becomes usable. A company's fiscal year ends in December, but its annual report appears only months later. A backtest that trades on the figures in January uses information that no investor had, which is why Fama and French wait until the following June.}}{59}{figure.caption.10}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.3}{\ignorespaces Micro-caps are most of the stocks but almost none of the money. Source: Fama and French (2008).}}{62}{figure.caption.11}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Two investments can share the same average and volatility yet differ in how often they crash. The four moments of a distribution. Each panel compares the standard normal distribution (solid) with one that differs in a single respect. (a)~The mean sets the center. (b)~The variance sets the spread. (c)~Negative skewness lengthens the left tail, so large losses are more likely than large gains of the same size. (d)~Higher kurtosis puts more weight in the tails: the normal distribution has kurtosis 3, the fat-tailed one shown has kurtosis 9. Panels (c) and (d) keep the mean and variance of the normal distribution.}}{77}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Beta measures how strongly a strategy follows the market, and alpha measures what it earns beyond that. Beta and alpha in a regression of a strategy's monthly excess returns on the market's. Beta is the slope of the fitted line; alpha is its height where the market's excess return is zero, the gap between the fitted line and a line with the same slope through the origin. Simulated data, with an alpha larger than any real strategy's so that it is visible.}}{80}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Diversification removes a company's own risk but not the market's. Splitting total variance into systematic and idiosyncratic parts with Equation~\eqref {eq:04-vardec-capm}. Each stock has a market beta of 1.2 and a residual standard deviation of 8\% a month, and the market's standard deviation is 4.5\% a month. Diversification leaves the systematic variance unchanged but divides the idiosyncratic variance by 100, so the systematic share, which equals the regression's $R^2$, rises from about 31\% to about 98\%. The numbers are illustrative.}}{82}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces In practice, riskier stocks have been rewarded less than the CAPM promises. The security market line. The CAPM predicts that average excess returns rise in a straight line with beta, passing through zero at a beta of zero and through the market's excess return at a beta of one (solid line). Tests that sort stocks into portfolios by beta typically find a flatter line with a positive intercept (dashed line and dots), so low-beta stocks earn more than predicted and high-beta stocks earn less (Black, Jensen and Scholes 1972). The numbers are illustrative.}}{84}{figure.caption.15}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Momentum ranks stocks on their past year, leaving out the latest month. The 12--1 momentum signal. Stocks are ranked on their return over the eleven months from $t-12$ to $t-1$; the most recent month is skipped; the portfolio is held for the month that follows.}}{102}{figure.caption.17}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces After a market fall, momentum ends up betting against the stocks most likely to rebound. Why momentum's beta flips (illustrative values). After a rising market the winners it holds are the high-beta stocks, so its net beta is positive. After a falling market the losers it sells short are the high-beta stocks, so its net beta turns strongly negative, and a sharp market rebound produces a large loss.}}{107}{figure.caption.18}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces A moving-average rule stays invested while the trend is up and steps aside when it turns down, but always a little late. The moving-average rule applied to a simulated price. The rule holds the asset (shaded) while a short average of the price (5 days) is above a long one (150 days). It rides the long rise and avoids most of the fall, but it acts only after each turn has begun.}}{117}{figure.caption.19}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces A breakout rule buys new highs and sells new lows. The breakout rule applied to the same simulated price as in Figure~\ref {fig:06-ma-rule}. The rule buys when the price rises above its highest level of the previous 50 days and sells when it falls below its lowest level; shaded periods are the days it holds the asset. Like the moving-average rule, it acts only after each turn has begun, but in this example it reacts sooner.}}{118}{figure.caption.20}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.1}{\ignorespaces ARCH(1): volatility rises only briefly}}{149}{figure.caption.24}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.2}{\ignorespaces GARCH(1,1): large days arrive in clusters}}{150}{figure.caption.25}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.3}{\ignorespaces EWMA lets an old shock fade gradually instead of dropping it all at once. Response of each estimator to a single 5\% move on day 10 when volatility is otherwise 1\% per day. The 22-day window raises its estimate by a fixed amount for exactly 22 days and then drops back abruptly when the shock leaves the window, even though nothing happened in the market that day. EWMA reacts more strongly at first, because yesterday receives the largest weight, and then lets the shock fade gradually.}}{152}{figure.caption.26}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.4}{\ignorespaces An option's losses are capped and its gains are not, which is why options are worth more when big moves are likely. Profit at expiry per barrel from a call and a put on oil, each with a strike of \$80 and a price of \$4. The call gains when the oil price rises above \$84, the strike plus the price paid, and the put gains when the oil price falls below \$76. If the oil price ends near \$80, either option expires and its holder loses only the \$4. Because the losses are capped and the gains are not, an option is worth more when large price moves in either direction are more likely.}}{157}{figure.caption.27}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.5}{\ignorespaces The stock market's ``fear gauge'' stays calm for long stretches and spikes in crises. Approximate path of the VIX, 1990--2025, drawn through its major peaks and troughs rather than daily data. The VIX measures the volatility of the S\&P~500 over the next 30 days implied by index option prices. It spends long periods near or below its average of about 20 and then spikes sharply during crises, the same volatility clustering seen in past returns, now visible in forward-looking prices. Exact daily values are available from FRED (series VIXCLS).}}{158}{figure.caption.28}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7.6}{\ignorespaces For a risk manager, underestimating risk is the costlier mistake, and QLIKE scores forecasts accordingly. Two robust loss functions, measured from their minimum. MSE treats a forecast that is too high and one that is too low symmetrically. QLIKE punishes a forecast that is too low far more than one that is too high: underestimating risk is the costlier mistake.}}{162}{figure.caption.29}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.1}{\ignorespaces What a 99\% one-day VaR of \$50 million means. Each bar is one day's profit or loss for a hypothetical trading book over one year, simulated from a normal distribution. The VaR line sits where losses are expected to fall below it on about 1 day in 100. Over 250 trading days the loss exceeds the VaR on 3 days, shown in orange, close to the 2.5 a correct model would produce.}}{170}{figure.caption.30}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8.2}{\ignorespaces VaR marks where the worst days begin, and expected shortfall measures how bad they are. VaR and expected shortfall for normal returns. The shaded tail holds the worst 1\% of days. The 99\% VaR, 2.33 standard deviations below zero, marks where that tail begins. The 99\% ES, 2.67 standard deviations, is the average loss on the days in the tail, so it is always larger than the VaR.}}{172}{figure.caption.31}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.1}{\ignorespaces The best available portfolios lie along a curved edge, and mixing them with Treasury bills does better still. The efficient frontier. Each gray dot is a single asset, and the curve shows the lowest volatility that any mix of the risky assets can achieve for each expected return. Only the upper half of the curve (solid) is efficient, because each portfolio on the lower half (dashed) is beaten by one directly above it with the same risk and a higher return. The open circle shows the same problem for a portfolio inside the curve: the arrow points to a frontier portfolio with the same volatility and a higher expected return. The two navy dots mark the global minimum-variance portfolio $\mathbf {w}_{MV}$ of Equation~\eqref {eq:12-minvar}, at the tip of the curve, and the tangency portfolio $\mathbf {w}_T$ of Equation~\eqref {eq:12-tangency}. When investors can also hold Treasury bills, the best combinations lie on the straight line from the origin through the tangency portfolio, whose slope is the highest attainable Sharpe ratio. A more risk-averse investor (high $\gamma $) holds part of wealth in bills, while a less risk-averse investor (low $\gamma $) borrows to hold more of the tangency portfolio. The numbers are illustrative.}}{189}{figure.caption.32}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.2}{\ignorespaces Even decades of data pin down an investment's average return only loosely. The standard error of an average return with 20\% volatility. With ten years of data it is 6.3\% a year; even 90 years leave it at 2.1\%. Differences in expected return between similar assets are far smaller than this, so the optimizer's most important input is mostly noise.}}{193}{figure.caption.33}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.3}{\ignorespaces The less alike two assets are, the more mixing them reduces risk. Frontiers for the two assets of Section~\ref {sec:12-frontier-derivation}, A (8\%) and B (6\%), each with 20\% volatility, at three correlations. All three curves pass through the two assets. The lower the correlation, the further the curve bends to the left, so the same expected return comes with less risk. The dots mark the minimum-variance portfolio, half in each asset, whose volatility rises from 14.1\% at a correlation of 0 to 17.3\% at 0.5 and 19.5\% at 0.9.}}{195}{figure.caption.34}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9.4}{\ignorespaces Past a certain point, the optimizer's apparent gains rest on bets that small estimation errors can reverse. The highest Sharpe ratio attainable from A and B as their correlation changes. Up to a correlation of 0.75, a higher correlation means less diversification, and the Sharpe ratio falls from 0.50 to 0.40, the ratio of A alone. Beyond 0.75 the optimizer shorts B to hedge A, and the Sharpe ratio rises again. That gain depends on knowing the expected returns exactly, which is why it rarely survives estimation error (Section~\ref {sec:12-estimation}).}}{195}{figure.caption.35}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.1}{\ignorespaces Adding more assets reduces risk only down to a floor set by how closely they move together. Volatility of an equally weighted portfolio of assets with 20\% volatility each. Adding assets helps only down to a floor set by the average correlation: 15.5\% at a correlation of 0.6 and 17.9\% at 0.8. With ten assets, as in the worked example, a rise from 0.6 to 0.8 lifts volatility from 16.0\% to 18.1\%.}}{211}{figure.caption.36}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10.2}{\ignorespaces Turbulence alone makes two markets look more closely linked than they are. The volatility bias in measured correlation. The link between the two markets never changes ($y = x + e$, with noise of 20\%), yet the measured correlation rises from 0.60 when $x$ has 15\% volatility to 0.83 when it has 30\%.}}{212}{figure.caption.37}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.1}{\ignorespaces When U.S. interest rates are higher than Japan's, the dollar's forward rate sits below its spot rate. USD/JPY spot rate and implied one-year forward rate, 2016--2026. It quotes the exchange rate as yen per dollar, the usual market convention, which is the inverse of the $S$ used in this chapter. A rise in the plotted line therefore means that the dollar has appreciated and the yen has depreciated, so $S$ has fallen. The forward rate comes from covered interest parity. It lies below the spot rate because U.S. interest rates exceed Japanese rates, and the gap widened after the Federal Reserve began raising rates in 2022.}}{224}{figure.caption.38}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.2}{\ignorespaces Uncovered interest parity says the risky foreign route and the safe home route should pay the same on average. Uncovered interest parity, with the numbers of the worked example: one year, a spot rate of \$0.70 per Australian dollar, 1\% in U.S. dollars and 5\% in Australian dollars. The Australian route is not hedged, so its dollar payoff depends on next year's spot rate $S_1$. UIP says that investors expect the Australian dollar to fall to the forward rate of about \$0.673, so the two routes have the same expected payoff. Only the U.S. route is certain.}}{229}{figure.caption.39}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.3}{\ignorespaces The carry trade profits if the high-interest currency holds its value and loses if it falls far enough. Profit on the worked example's carry trade against the spot rate a year later. The trade earns \$40,000 if the Australian dollar is unchanged, breaks even at the UIP forecast of \$0.673, and loses \$65,000 if the currency falls 10\%.}}{231}{figure.caption.40}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11.4}{\ignorespaces In practice, high-interest currencies have tended to rise rather than fall, the opposite of what the theory predicts. Three readings of the Fama regression. The horizontal axis is $i^* - i$, the negative of the differential in Equation~\eqref {eq:06-fama}, so each line has slope $-b$. Under UIP ($b = 1$), a high-interest currency falls by its interest advantage. Under a random walk ($b = 0$), it does not move, and under the typical estimate ($b \approx -0.9$, Froot and Thaler 1990), it rises. The carry trade's expected return is the vertical distance above the UIP line. At an advantage of 3 points, it is zero under UIP, 3\% under a random walk, and 5.7\% under the typical estimate.}}{233}{figure.caption.41}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.1}{\ignorespaces Long-term rates are usually above short-term rates, and the rare exceptions draw headlines. Long-term and short-term interest rates in the United States, 2000--2025 (top), and the term spread (bottom), defined as $i^{\mathrm {long}} - i^{\mathrm {short}}$, where $i^{\mathrm {long}}$ is the 10-year Treasury yield and $i^{\mathrm {short}}$ is the 3-month Treasury bill rate. The data are annual averages, and the shaded bands are recessions. The term spread was large in the early 2010s, when short rates were near zero, and it turned negative in 2023 and 2024, when short rates rose above long rates.}}{241}{figure.caption.42}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.2}{\ignorespaces Forward rates have usually predicted higher interest rates than actually arrived. Forward rates and the yield curve in the United States, 2000--2025. Panel~(a) shows the 1-year and 2-year Treasury yields and the forward rate they imply for the following year, $f = (1 + y^{(2)})^2 / (1 + y^{(1)}) - 1$, where $y^{(n)}$ is the $n$-year yield. When the 2-year yield is above the 1-year yield, the forward rate is above both. Panel~(b) compares each forward rate with the 1-year yield that actually prevailed a year later. If forward rates were unbiased forecasts, the two lines would differ only by random errors. Instead, the forward rate was usually too high, most visibly from 2009 to 2015, when it kept predicting rises that did not come, and it badly missed the rapid rise in 2022. The data are approximate annual averages.}}{242}{figure.caption.43}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.3}{\ignorespaces A forward rate from the worked example. A two-year yield of 5\% is equivalent to earning the one-year yield of 4\% in the first year and a rate locked in today, the forward rate of 6\%, in the second.}}{246}{figure.caption.44}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {12.4}{\ignorespaces A steep yield curve can be a forecast of rising rates, extra pay for holding long bonds, or both. Two readings of a steep yield curve. Forward rates above short rates can mean that short rates will rise, as the expectations hypothesis says, or that long bonds will earn more than short ones, a term premium. The tests in this chapter measure how much of each.}}{248}{figure.caption.45}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.1}{\ignorespaces Every trader who wants to deal at once pays a little more to buy than she receives to sell. A limit order book. Sellers' asks sit above the midpoint and buyers' bids below it. A buyer who wants to trade at once pays the best ask, \$50.02; a seller receives the best bid, \$49.98. The gap of \$0.04, 8 basis points of the \$50.00 midpoint, is the bid--ask spread.}}{260}{figure.caption.46}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.2}{\ignorespaces The more a strategy trades, the faster its profit disappears as costs rise. Net return against one-way trading cost, from the turnover and gross returns in the table. Each line falls at a rate of four times the strategy's turnover; it crosses zero at the break-even cost. Value's line is nearly flat and crosses zero off the chart, at about 4\%.}}{268}{figure.caption.47}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {13.3}{\ignorespaces Bigger orders cost more per dollar, though less than in proportion to their size. Square-root market impact for a stock with 2\% daily volatility, from Equation~\eqref {eq:17-impact} with $Y = 1$. Ten times the order size costs about three times as much per dollar, not ten times; but total impact cost, per dollar times dollars, still rises faster than the order.}}{269}{figure.caption.48}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14.1}{\ignorespaces Low-beta stocks have done better, and high-beta stocks worse, than the CAPM predicts. The security market line in the worked example. The CAPM line runs from the risk-free rate of 3\% through the market at a beta of 1. Stock A lies above it (positive alpha) and stock B below it (negative alpha), so the line through A and B is flatter than the CAPM predicts.}}{279}{figure.caption.49}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {14.2}{\ignorespaces Betting against beta borrows to scale up low-beta stocks and scales down high-beta ones until the market's moves cancel. Building a BAB portfolio from the worked example. Per dollar of capital, it holds \$1.67 of the low-beta stock ($\beta _L = 0.6$) and shorts \$0.67 of the high-beta stock ($\beta _H = 1.5$). Each leg then carries a beta exposure of 1, in opposite directions, so the portfolio's beta is zero.}}{281}{figure.caption.50}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15.1}{\ignorespaces If only significant results are published, the journals show only the lucky draws. Publication bias. One hundred signals with no true power produce t-statistics spread around zero. Only the few in the shaded tails, about 2.5 on each side, look significant and get published. The published record shows only the tails.}}{293}{figure.caption.51}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15.2}{\ignorespaces Published patterns lost more than half of their returns after publication. Post-publication decay in McLean and Pontiff (2016). With in-sample returns set to 100, the average predictor earned 74 between the end of its original sample and publication, and 42 after publication.}}{299}{figure.caption.53}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.1}{\ignorespaces Profit at expiration of the four basic option positions. The stock trades at \$100 today, every option has a strike price of \$100 and three months to expiry, the call costs \$4.50 and the put \$3.50. The dots mark three outcomes: the stock falls to \$90, stays at \$100 or rises to \$120. Buyers (navy, left) pay the premium, risk only that amount and gain from a large move. Sellers (red, right) receive the premium, gain at most that amount and bear the loss from a large move. Each seller's line is the mirror image of the buyer's, because the buyer's gain is the seller's loss. The fund manager in the story is in the top-left panel, and the bank that sold her the call is in the top-right panel.}}{307}{figure.caption.54}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.2}{\ignorespaces An option's value curve bends, which is why its hedge must keep changing. The Greeks of the call in the story, with a strike of \$100 and a 4\% interest rate. The solid navy curve is the option's Black--Scholes value with 20\% volatility and three months to expiry. Delta is its slope, 0.56 at a stock price of \$100, and gamma is how fast that slope changes: the curve bends, so delta rises from 0.18 at \$90 to 0.87 at \$110. Vega is how much the curve rises when volatility rises: at 30\% volatility, the dotted curve, the price at \$100 is \$1.97 higher. Theta is how much the curve falls as time passes: with one month left, the gray curve, the price at \$100 is \$2.02 lower. As expiry approaches, the curve falls toward the payoff at expiry.}}{314}{figure.caption.58}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.3}{\ignorespaces A hedged option seller earns a little on quiet days and loses on big moves in either direction. The bank's delta-hedged profit or loss over one day, by Equation~\eqref {eq:14-pnl}, for the call in the story. The bank earns \$0.022 if the stock is unchanged, breaks even on a move of about \$1.05 either way, and loses \$0.156 on a \$3 move. The loss grows with the square of the move.}}{318}{figure.caption.62}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.4}{\ignorespaces Hedging more often shrinks the error, but only slowly. Hedging error in the Black--Scholes world, by the Kamal--Derman approximation, for a one-month at-the-money option at 20\% volatility. Hedging weekly leaves an error of about 41\% of the premium, daily about 18\%, and four times a day about 9\%. Each quadrupling of the frequency halves the error.}}{319}{figure.caption.63}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.1}{\ignorespaces Implied volatility is usually higher than the volatility that follows. Panel A compares the yearly average of the VIX, the volatility priced into one-month S\&P 500 options, with the realized volatility of the S\&P 500 in the same year. Panel B shows the difference. A seller of index options gains in years when it is positive and loses when it is negative. Implied volatility was higher in all but a few years since 1990, by about 3 percentage points on average, but in 2008 and 2020 the market moved far more than option prices had allowed for. Numbers are approximate yearly averages.}}{329}{figure.caption.65}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {17.2}{\ignorespaces A covered call keeps almost all of the market's losses but caps its gains. A one-month at-the-money covered call against the index alone, with the index at 100, a 4\% interest rate and 20\% implied volatility, as in the worked example, at which the call is worth \$2.47. The premium caps the gain and cushions the loss by the same amount, so the covered call keeps almost all of the index's downside.}}{332}{figure.caption.66}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.1}{\ignorespaces Bias and variance. Each dot is a forecast from the same model estimated on a different training sample, and the bullseye is the truth. Bias is how far the dots' average lies from the bullseye; variance is how widely the dots are scattered. The best model has both low (top left), but reducing one usually raises the other.}}{348}{figure.caption.67}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.2}{\ignorespaces The bias--variance trade-off. The horizontal axis measures model complexity, roughly the number of parameters the model estimates; the vertical axis measures the model's expected forecast error on new data. Moving right, bias (dashed) falls and variance (dotted) rises, while the irreducible error (flat line) stays the same. Total error, their sum, is lowest at an intermediate complexity marked ``best.''}}{349}{figure.caption.68}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.3}{\ignorespaces A regression tree of depth two. Each stock answers the questions from the top down and ends in a leaf, whose forecast is the average return of the training stocks that ended there. The second question is asked only of stocks with positive momentum, so the effect of size depends on momentum: an interaction. The numbers are illustrative.}}{353}{figure.caption.69}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.4}{\ignorespaces Bagging and random forests. Each of $B$ trees is grown deep on its own bootstrap sample of the training data, and the forecast is the average of all the trees. In a random forest, each split may also choose only from a random subset of the predictors, which makes the trees differ more.}}{356}{figure.caption.70}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.5}{\ignorespaces Gradient boosting. The forecast starts from the average return. Each small tree is fitted to the errors left by the forecast so far, and only a fraction of it, set by the learning rate, is added. The final forecast is the sum of many small corrections.}}{357}{figure.caption.71}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.6}{\ignorespaces Gradient descent on the loss $L(x) = x^2 - x - 2$. Starting from $x_0 = 3$ with a learning rate of 0.25, each step moves downhill by a quarter of the slope, and the search closes in on the minimum at $x = 0.5$.}}{359}{figure.caption.72}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.7}{\ignorespaces From regression to a neural network. Left: a logistic regression drawn as a network, with four inputs, one weight on each arrow, and an output neuron that applies the sigmoid function. Right: adding a hidden layer of neurons, each with its own weighted sum and ReLU activation, lets the network learn nonlinear relationships and interactions.}}{361}{figure.caption.74}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.8}{\ignorespaces Monthly out-of-sample $R^2$ for individual U.S. stocks, 1987--2016, from Gu, Kelly and Xiu (2020). Trees and neural networks roughly doubled the $R^2$ of a three-characteristic OLS model. OLS on all 920 covariates, at $-3.46\%$, was far worse than forecasting zero.}}{364}{figure.caption.75}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {18.9}{\ignorespaces Double descent. To the left of the interpolation threshold, test error follows the U shape of Figure~\ref {fig:09-biasvar}. Near the threshold the model can fit the training data exactly, and test error peaks. Beyond it, among the many models that fit the training data exactly, estimation picks the one with the smallest coefficients, and test error can fall again. The curve is stylized; whether the second descent appears, and how far it falls, depends on the problem.}}{368}{figure.caption.79}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19.1}{\ignorespaces A fair test lets every model see only the past. Rolling-origin evaluation. At each origin every model sees the same data up to that month and forecasts the next one; the origin then moves forward a month. No model ever sees the month it is forecasting.}}{378}{figure.caption.80}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {19.2}{\ignorespaces A small forecasting gain takes decades of monthly data to prove. The expected Diebold--Mariano statistic for the worked example's 5\% gain, as the evaluation sample grows. It reaches 1.96 only after about 384 months; a two-year post-cutoff window gives about 0.5.}}{378}{figure.caption.81}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\addvspace {10\p@ }
